title: Labels and contexts
tags: [[The operation log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Operations are grouped by nested labels (name, description, parent) opened as contexts by scripts, commands and the GUI — passed along as an id, so leaving a context is just not passing it on.

Built in October 2026 ([[The operation log]]): labels, contexts and commands are in place in the daemon,
the CLI, the GUI and the shipped scripts ([[Log endpoints]]), undo and redo offer them
([[Undo and redo by label]]), and the [[log panel]] shows their tree.

A ''label'' is `{id, name, description, parent, created}`. Each operation carries its
''innermost'' label; the others are its ancestors, found by following the parents. Same
information as every label on every operation while contexts nest, without copying three
ids onto each of a bulk write's 50 000 operations.

!! Contexts

A context is a label that the writes made inside it carry. It is ''not daemon state'':

* `mf label begin "name" "description"` creates a label, child of the current context, and prints it as `<repo uuid>:<label id>` ([[mf label begin]]);
* the caller exports it (`METAFOLDER_LABEL=<repo>:<id>`), and every `mf` started meanwhile sends it (`X-Metafolder-Label`) with its requests to that repository — a label id means nothing in another one, where it is not sent;
* leaving the context is ceasing to export it — the end of a subshell or a function's `local`. A script that crashes leaves nothing open behind it.

Every command opens its own label, a child of the context: `mf metarecord -q '…' field
set …` is a label named after the command line and described by its query, so the log
shows what was asked, not only what was written. It is opened ''at the command's first
write'', not when it starts: the client draws a key for the command and sends it with
every request (`X-Metafolder-Command`, with the label's name and description,
percent-encoded); the daemon opens the label at the first write carrying the key, and the
command's later writes find it by the key. A command that only reads — most of them, and
some read through `POST` — opens nothing, and the client never has to guess which of its
requests write.

!! Who opens which

* A shipped tagging script: one label for the run, one per answer (its going back is reverting the last answer's label — no head noted by hand, no rollback), one per command inside ([[Writing a GUI script]]).
* The GUI: one label per command. The shell tells the daemon proxy when a command starts and ends, and every request in between carries its key; a command run from inside another is part of it. The proxy knows which command runs, not which call each made: two commands running at once — a long reconcile and a field edited meanwhile — share a label. A script launched from the GUI (`script:run`) runs in a label of its own, passed on as `METAFOLDER_LABEL`; a plain `!` line in none, each `mf` it runs being a command anyway.
* The GUI's ''work context'': a command opens a context that every later user operation carries until it is closed — naming the work under way, so it can be looked at or reverted whole. The watcher's operations do not carry it.
* The watcher: one label per flush ("watcher flush"), under a ''watcher root'' ("watcher") that only the daemon can write under: a client naming it, or a label inside it, as its context or as a new label's parent is refused. The ''origin'' stays on each operation, set by the daemon ([[Operations and their origin]]) — not read off the root as first proposed: a trashing's `trash` origin must survive inside any context, and undo and retention read the operation.

!! The log panel

Operations grouped by their outermost label (name and description); a group opens onto
its child labels, down to single operations. Consecutive watcher labels are folded into
one line, or they would drown the rest.

!! Deferred

The GUI's work context is secondary (user, 12 October 2026), and not part of the first
build ([[The GUI's work context]]): whether it belongs to a workspace or to the whole GUI, and whether it survives a
restart, is decided when it is built. So is renaming or describing a label after the fact.
